---
title: Random rooms
---
Scatter rectangles over a solid map, keep the ones that don’t overlap, and join them with corridors [[t:47-51 s:5]].

## The rule
```text
repeat for a fixed number of attempts:
    pick a random rectangle
    carve it as a room, unless it comes within a tile of an earlier room
sort the rooms left to right
join each room to the one before it with an L-shaped corridor
```

The talk’s first technique is one that anyone who has done a roguelike development tutorial has seen [[t:44-46]]. Its demo sorts the rooms by their left edge and joins each to the one before it, center to center [[c01:57-78]].

## Step through it
@@steps limit=6

## Controls
@@control attempts
How many rectangles are tried. The demo’s loop runs a fixed 20 times, and a rectangle that overlaps is discarded without another try [[c01:20-54]].
@@control min_size
The smallest value a room’s width and height are drawn from [[c01:22-27]]. A room covers one tile more than the value each way, because the rectangle type the demo uses includes both of its ends [[own]].
@@control max_size
The largest such value. The demo writes it as an upper end of 10, which its random call leaves out [[own]].

## What it produces, measured
@@measured

Every seed gives {{c01.regions.max}} region and {{c01.dead_ends.max}} dead ends [[run:c01.regions.max c01.dead_ends.max]]: each room is joined to the one before it, and a corridor ends inside a room [[own]]. Of 20 attempts, {{c01.rooms.median}} place a room at the median, and never fewer than {{c01.rooms.min}} [[run:c01.rooms.median c01.rooms.min]].

## Where it fails
@@fails

Its attempts are a fixed 20, and its corridors one for each room after the first [[c01:20-21 c01:67-78]].

## What it combines with
@@combines

The talk’s demos of erosion and of a prefab placed in a room both start by building a map of random rooms [[c08:21-83 c14:21-125]].

